.TH IOM_WAIT 3
.SH NAME
iom_wait \- wait for I/O events on an I/O multiplexing context
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <iom.h>
.PP
.BI "int iom_wait(iomux_t* " c ", int64* " s ", unsigned int* " revents ", unsigned long " timeout ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iom_wait
waits for I/O events on the file descriptors associated with the I/O multiplexing context
.IR c .
.PP
The function blocks until either an event occurs or the specified
.I timeout
(in milliseconds) expires.
.PP
If an event occurs, it is stored in the memory locations pointed to by
.I s
(the file descriptor that triggered the event) and
.I revents
(a bitmask of occurred events, e.g.,
.BR IOM_READ ,
.BR IOM_WRITE ,
.BR IOM_ERROR ).
.PP
The function supports multiple threads calling
.B iom_wait
simultaneously on the same context. It uses internal synchronization primitives
(semaphores or condition variables) to ensure that only one thread performs the actual
system call (epoll or kqueue) while others wait for the results.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.TP
.B 1
An event was successfully retrieved.
.TP
.B 0
The timeout expired without any events occurring.
.TP
.B -1
An error occurred;
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.TP
.B -2
The context
.I c
has been aborted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iom_init (3),
.BR iom_add (3),
.BR epoll (7),
.BR kqueue (2)